From 780977fe0b7661460e92014b6337daa7f4bb5121 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: alisachen-google <280552753+alisachen-google@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 02:02:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [TPU/PD] Pull an evicted prefix back from the decode engine's
 HiCache over Raiden

In a P/D pair the prefill engine's HBM and host tiers are private and small.
When a session returns after other traffic evicted its prefix, the prefill
recomputes it: about 6 s at 128k tokens and 17 s at 256k on tpu7x with
gpt-oss-120b. The decode engine already holds that KV: every prefilled
request lands in decode HBM, and with --disaggregation-decode-enable-radix-cache
and HiCache the decode keeps finished requests in its radix tree and writes
them through to its host pool.

This change lets a prefill rank read such a prefix back from its decode peer.
HiCache on each engine stays the only directory; Raiden is used only for D2H,
H2D and the device-to-device transfer. No store process, no registry, no
host-to-host DMA.

- sglang_tpu/disaggregation/raiden/serde.py: PREFIX_REQ, PREFIX_BLOCKS and
  PREFIX_RELEASE messages on the existing per-rank control channel.
- sglang_tpu/disaggregation/raiden/peer_prefix.py: PeerPrefixMixin on
  RaidenKVManager. Decode role: match the tokens in the radix tree, lock the
  nodes, restore host pages to HBM through init_load_back when the model has
  no sliding window, register_read the device pages beyond the prefill's
  local match (plus the sliding-window pages of the last window), reply, and
  unlock on PREFIX_RELEASE, when Raiden reports the read, or on timeout.
  Prefill role: send the request, start_read into HBM pages, poll
  completion, release. The listener thread only enqueues; tree operations
  run on the scheduler thread.
- sglang_tpu/pd_peer_prefix.py: scheduler integration behind
  SGLANG_TPU_PEER_PREFIX=1. Decode: process_decode_queue serves pending
  requests. Prefill: _add_request_to_queue starts a fetch when the local
  match has no host hit and the gap is at least SGLANG_TPU_PEER_PREFIX_MIN_TOKENS;
  pop_bootstrapped holds the request, allocates the pages with alloc_extend,
  starts the read, and when every rank's read settled inserts the pages into
  UnifiedRadixCache (prev_prefix_len = local match; SWA evicted seqlen =
  window start) before the request is scheduled. Stage transitions use
  MIN/MAX all-reduces over the attention-TP group, including whether a pull
  can start, so every rank inserts the same key in the same step. Reads in
  flight per rank stay under half a max-size request so they never starve
  the P/D transfers that share the same host staging. The local prefix is
  not locked while a pull is in flight; the commit re-matches it and gives
  up if it moved. Pages a pull holds are reported to the scheduler's idle
  pool-invariant check as in flight. Any failure or timeout falls back to
  recompute; pages with a read still in flight drain until Raiden settles.
- sglang_tpu/patches.py: install the hook.

Limits: a hybrid sliding-window model gets a hit only while the decode still
holds the prefix and its last window in HBM, because the decode's host copy
is full-attention only. A prefill request with a partial host-tier hit does
not use the peer. One decode engine per prefill rank, learned from its pull
requests.

Tests: 13 CPU tests in test_peer_prefix.py drive both roles with fake
engines and a fake tree: serve and lock, miss below the threshold, restore
wait, hybrid window pages, lease release, failure and timeout, the decode
hook, hook pull-insert-release, abandon and drain, in-flight pages counted
as uncached, a mismatched reply, a failing commit, and the read budget.
Live results on a tpu7x 1P/1D pair are in the PR description.
